  "worked_solution": "Let's examine our vector spaces.\n\n- ${R}_{1000}[t]$ and ${\\text{M}}_{200\\times 500}(R)$ are finite-dimensional. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{1000}[t])=1\\,000+1=1\\,001,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{200\\times 500}(R))=200\\cdot 500=100\\,000\n$$\n\n \nare both finite numbers.\n\n- $C$ is also finite-dimensional. To understand why, we first note that the following set of complex numbers is linearly independent:\n\n$$\n{1,i}\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have that \n\n$$\na\\cdot 1+b\\cdot i=0\n$$\n\nif and only if $a=0$ and $b=0.$ Since our vector space is also spanned by this linearly independent subset, we have that $\\text{dim}(C)=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"",
